#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ab2e12-2968-573c-b80a-dc4e9677da96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(81)/(64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269312} {:answer/id #uuid "424e9d4e-6115-5d2a-a9e3-39eba71c5354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269313} {:answer/id #uuid "1085e7fa-fd5f-5ef4-91e2-f641d8d93106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269314} {:answer/id #uuid "e92f9dae-bf5b-577a-801a-6a2b78d5b474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269315} {:answer/id #uuid "e05a63b3-8b26-5563-89b6-f116fc62f990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e92f9dae-bf5b-577a-801a-6a2b78d5b474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269315} :answer-field/id #uuid "e830c85f-e450-5ebe-a1f6-0f6b131915ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274137}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3c8ab44-204b-5d29-bcb7-af809bc21a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-100273" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{3}-4(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed821a32-9ed7-5b47-80be-e0e68acf4b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(7)x^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269492} {:answer/id #uuid "484ce149-1d06-5e8e-a2ee-331dc1d54048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (35)/(x^{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269493} {:answer/id #uuid "31e5f2d9-b1e5-533b-b086-a7d02360fdde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(35)/(x^{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269494} {:answer/id #uuid "4f20235a-a1a0-5a11-9213-31b5a3bac07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269495} {:answer/id #uuid "09055399-0198-5e3b-a47c-906443a5a4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(5)x^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f20235a-a1a0-5a11-9213-31b5a3bac07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269495} :answer-field/id #uuid "0327b1b4-a06d-5bbb-81f3-d1652a787303" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274173}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34bf4bc2-4f77-5ec2-b028-851ecd7a1784" :question/math-academy-id "q-102185" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{4x},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4x}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-1){x}^{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffc1d505-17e4-5b0f-9150-506f9edb3350" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6be22dc2-5de7-5aff-91b3-46f8b639bfc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269517} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a8b616-fd24-589f-93ce-1fa4cfcec7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269518} {:answer/id #uuid "6513e7f3-f1be-5c76-ae22-866babda23ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28{x}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269519} {:answer/id #uuid "0a0982b6-5edd-5c1c-9710-d584e3d56ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{x}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269520} {:answer/id #uuid "7188eebb-f8c7-5a64-ab34-1ee25ae22488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9a8b616-fd24-589f-93ce-1fa4cfcec7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269518} :answer-field/id #uuid "64872068-da72-5afa-8ca8-1851ee145e6c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274178}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da3e499-592f-56ab-a8b2-fa769f0b592f" :question/math-academy-id "q-10289" :question/problem "Find the derivative of  $f(x)=4{x}^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6{x}^{6-1} \\\\  & =24{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efe247d9-f575-536c-9009-a37f17dcb1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x^{4} + 21x^{2} + (27)/(x^{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269607} {:answer/id #uuid "36540e26-c55e-5479-adeb-efc84008f121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(x^{3}) + (9x^{− 1/4})/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269608} {:answer/id #uuid "b57edc1a-c3f5-5f1a-9d6e-6ccf205e5a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(x^{3}) − (9x^{1/4})/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269609} {:answer/id #uuid "77104ce4-dc4d-5ee7-9862-68a27013f724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(x^{2}) − 9x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269610} {:answer/id #uuid "220ee788-eb1c-5e30-950c-04de48071e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "220ee788-eb1c-5e30-950c-04de48071e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269611} :answer-field/id #uuid "3efdd5ba-332b-5c35-9145-6bd75812a0d6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274196}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f25ae3e-b1ef-5b98-82be-6340c3c3c87e" :question/math-academy-id "q-10726" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=5{x}^{15}-4{x}^{8}+7{x}^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{15}-4{x}^{8}+7{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{15})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{15})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})+7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 15{x}^{(15-1)}-4\\cdot 8{x}^{(8-1)}+7\\cdot 5{x}^{(5-1)} \\\\  & =75{x}^{14}-32{x}^{7}+35{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e455de-4c09-541b-934a-40eebfda2b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35{x}^{4}-6x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269612} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ccee72-8a32-5160-934e-0ea278535f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25x^{4} + 21x^{2} + (27)/(x^{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269613} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc28582-fac4-52e2-9288-37cfa395fc10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(x^{3}) + (9x^{− 1/4})/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269614} {:answer/id #uuid "927f366f-fad3-514d-90d1-330c0728bafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(x^{3}) − (9x^{1/4})/(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269615} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc05c3f-89ce-550a-b478-e3bca34f51e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(x^{2}) − 9x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82e455de-4c09-541b-934a-40eebfda2b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35{x}^{4}-6x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269612} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd16dd66-d6d5-5c67-b8e8-5e61e2a44447" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274197}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36a4921a-bbad-5ced-a8e4-cc7bcc79bde0" :question/math-academy-id "q-10730" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}-5x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{5})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5{x}^{(5-1)}-3\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}-5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =35{x}^{4}-6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc0ce2c2-36b9-526b-93d6-31bf223ef326" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de4becab-1d19-5348-8c32-5ebf12288639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=(0)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269870} {:answer/id #uuid "07a66cab-d642-5cb8-bfae-d7c7994a0ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=(0)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269871} {:answer/id #uuid "5d918a45-94ca-5011-bb15-c914ecbbb084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269872} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6130c4-3131-52ea-9497-968c747b6aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=(0)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d918a45-94ca-5011-bb15-c914ecbbb084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269872} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d857887-05e0-5643-ae47-c7508e7e1bf8" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65d26569-d024-5df5-80e7-ebdb725c458d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112805" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $5{x}^{3}$ and $-15{x}^{2}$ is $5{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68315433-c3de-52bb-9726-56247343fd82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269889} {:answer/id #uuid "5daebc2c-2f18-5380-9dcd-2f8a15915f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269890} {:answer/id #uuid "189a3e6b-7417-566b-875f-e33bf31fe23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269891} {:answer/id #uuid "9107f79c-8ee3-5619-b6c0-16fbf57a2869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269892} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7f6e58-c39f-5597-8f9a-76280b9abe92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9107f79c-8ee3-5619-b6c0-16fbf57a2869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269892} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb4e9a27-c50e-51ae-b42a-f1e264f37d15" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274253}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e4c35d0-80ea-555f-a42d-fa766d0cba07" :question/math-academy-id "q-112867" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+10?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{-5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67915b9-d3ad-5028-96ad-a45db46254f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269894} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb64211-4410-5663-bd84-281a8787bad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269895} {:answer/id #uuid "18d885f8-4fe3-5e0e-93d5-3e5dab645db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269896} {:answer/id #uuid "fc481543-ca19-5317-a0ce-94d4f2e65c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269897} {:answer/id #uuid "62f934d5-5b8c-5451-8c42-9e3f983c4321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f934d5-5b8c-5451-8c42-9e3f983c4321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269898} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a1e573-580d-56e8-a91f-d31e3f8253c7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274254}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284a91b3-0aac-5ff1-be86-b1f45987e7b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112871" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-12{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}-3x+10?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10,$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(5)$ is a factor of $10$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4856d144-ebeb-5b8d-853d-a286e7892e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (4)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269899} {:answer/id #uuid "8270bb55-c8af-5692-934f-b5540b39e488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269900} {:answer/id #uuid "ff62be7c-5fd8-5d74-ac65-6ca0a9ff951b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269901} {:answer/id #uuid "84125245-aa56-5c7e-b3f3-f8baf067f9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269902} {:answer/id #uuid "b2864952-7fe6-5f74-928c-0192428c92f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2864952-7fe6-5f74-928c-0192428c92f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269903} :answer-field/id #uuid "d377989a-6b56-556c-b57d-3fedf50fcee7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274255}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25b2159c-942d-5822-b9a6-b71a06f24251" :question/math-academy-id "q-112878" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $4$ does not take the above form. The value $4$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{4}{1},$ and the numerator $(4)$ is not a factor of $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8058df30-04de-555c-adfa-61fbf2b0ff31" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3762fb88-4bad-5745-b052-42e8c760c9c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\frac{1}{2z})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269929} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6fb09-9ed7-5b81-8026-b546b45f6c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269930} {:answer/id #uuid "f53e8123-80ec-581a-9a0e-33c2798cb930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\frac{1}{2z})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269931} {:answer/id #uuid "e61e2c8e-1643-5370-8f99-e931ad39ee39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{1}{2z})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269932} {:answer/id #uuid "46a0e5c3-8d0a-5c1c-b30f-4aa985222927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2z})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6fb09-9ed7-5b81-8026-b546b45f6c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269930} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce87d369-1a0d-529f-bb5f-3f1df21bdc22" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274261}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "783bfb90-6184-5e8a-b664-e0e6d1dc5933" :question/math-academy-id "q-113228" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{3}{2z}=p$ and $\\frac{2}{z}=q,$ what is $p-q?$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\np-q=\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2z$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2z}-\\frac{4}{2z} & = \\\\ \\frac{3-4}{2z} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2z} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dbbb15-a4f5-5775-a204-1e7a8e5e5d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269949} {:answer/id #uuid "d31e7d00-3683-58f2-b62a-cbb82f144f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269950} {:answer/id #uuid "252d23ea-2f6d-526c-8a3a-56d42fe25bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269951} {:answer/id #uuid "93400ae8-5740-5163-84a7-bb92c1089f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269952} {:answer/id #uuid "b2323986-56cd-5437-a9cf-260085d15a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30dbbb15-a4f5-5775-a204-1e7a8e5e5d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269949} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbdb407-ea87-5d76-92d5-e67f4b3b2a99" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274265}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae19e4b0-11a7-5a6d-8c85-2a0f9d7eb465" :question/math-academy-id "q-113311" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $n(m-4)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{n}{m-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{n\\cdot n}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m}{n(m-4)}+\\frac{{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5m+{n}^{2}}{n(m-4)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "add45606-39f6-5a61-9535-867d2950d183" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dee46692-b9d3-54af-bafe-88d817c6b60f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270012} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7dc11f-41d1-5542-a10f-043bcaddaaaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270013} {:answer/id #uuid "21a9ca1f-6bdf-5dc7-b912-20c240641efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270014} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ebf84d-6fb6-5907-8d01-63a1aaed3bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270015} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd4558f-d339-544a-9b91-184cf2e9a748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7dc11f-41d1-5542-a10f-043bcaddaaaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270013} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d278a1c-cfb4-548e-98e3-ea863b62f627" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274278}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e26de700-a75c-58a7-9db4-45270d8fe36b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113737" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {x}^{-11}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-11+1}}{-11+1})+C \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-10}}{-10})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot {x}^{-10}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "562cb518-7165-5b24-854f-d0ce30dde677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((4c)+1,\\frac{5d}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270112} {:answer/id #uuid "8caf30e3-a8ef-5bea-b1f3-580b3b1c0a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4c,\\frac{5d}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270113} {:answer/id #uuid "648d61e1-5071-5d73-974f-9874ed228f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((4c)+2,\\frac{5d}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270114} {:answer/id #uuid "82dd5d05-9017-5884-ba8a-536aedecaccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((4c)-2,\\frac{5d}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270115} {:answer/id #uuid "334bc486-afc7-580b-8a46-795c597c3739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((4c)-1,\\frac{5d}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8caf30e3-a8ef-5bea-b1f3-580b3b1c0a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4c,\\frac{5d}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270113} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd38ab8b-a870-529e-9ed9-6bec7f40d1aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274298}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1b6dcd8-b86b-57fe-bcef-873b76734928" :question/math-academy-id "q-113962" :question/problem "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(2c,-d)$ and $(6c,6d).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(2c,-d)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(6c,6d)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2c+6c}{2},\\frac{-d+6d}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{2},\\frac{5d}{2}) \\\\  & =(4c,\\frac{5d}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db0f4fee-52ce-59ea-a878-1cc3251e9c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db0f4fee-52ce-59ea-a878-1cc3251e9c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257293} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebf1136-0ad1-5236-b788-da4efd706743" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257292}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ae8950d-1f55-5b0d-b4d8-6562835c999a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118839" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $A(8,-3,-1)$ and $B(12,5,-2)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(8,-3,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(12,5,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(12-8)^{2}+(5-(-3))^{2}+(-2-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e178c7-bdb4-5139-a20c-3e17de51ee11" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df561d3-5704-567e-9bea-30eaabf51a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0df561d3-5704-567e-9bea-30eaabf51a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257309} :answer-field/id #uuid "47bec473-b654-5bf5-8f7f-7ce0b93b538f" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee79302d-cad7-5427-88cd-f0908fb97959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee79302d-cad7-5427-88cd-f0908fb97959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257311} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8f41b8-b188-56fc-ab1f-eefeefda3503" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257310}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a478ded4-a3e7-57e8-9ba2-a2eb8cb92b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-118955" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(12w,2w,-4w)$ and $B(7w,-6w,2w).$ Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $30\\sqrt{5},$ find the possible values of $w.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(12w,2w,-4w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(7w,-6w,2w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(7w-12w)^{2}+(-6w-2w)^{2}+(2w-(-4w))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5w)^{2}+(-8w)^{2}+(6w)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{w}^{2}+64{w}^{2}+36{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{125}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=30\\sqrt{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5}\\,|w| & =30\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |w| & =6 \\\\ w & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88bd4db-8477-5822-9cda-a608e78039e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5372e635-bed7-5aa8-812a-07042f0f0d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270367} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd0a546-b13b-52c1-8ecb-8de88634a9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270368} {:answer/id #uuid "10415a47-ea0e-5315-9a66-a5ba89eb3b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270369} {:answer/id #uuid "2512a5f6-c213-545c-bfe5-d69fa91ea9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270370} {:answer/id #uuid "d6361c27-5c8b-5783-b658-599f7e626f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{66}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6361c27-5c8b-5783-b658-599f7e626f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{66}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270371} :answer-field/id #uuid "44573d06-4997-5ca5-aebb-5a6bdcce496b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274349}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f19f56b-b91e-5f5c-acd5-5bfff04f8d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-119346" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a058d9b800decdb32fdd09715f1bebab03b4a12c7e6119d6c45bbb170ce640b0.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "The length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is equal to the distance between $P$ and $Q.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $P({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $Q({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ have coordinates ${{(6,\\,0,\\,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(-1,4,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\sqrt{(-1-6)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}+(2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{66}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57bae420-d529-5b9d-a306-37127c523be6" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule of Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8511eba-9796-5997-8f6d-2729ff4bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270517} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3981fc-1a10-5f69-a857-f56af4c640c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270518} {:answer/id #uuid "368b545e-81c6-5d9f-a10b-d542095f369e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270519} {:answer/id #uuid "0877e626-bac4-5c19-ab87-69e6f2f758e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270520} {:answer/id #uuid "ff79579d-dffc-5149-b6b2-18e6741daf17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff79579d-dffc-5149-b6b2-18e6741daf17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270521} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c219ba8-28bc-5422-b750-44aa3b67d656" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274379}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfa71c25-63e9-530b-b8d9-c3e3f8a5bfb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122144" :question/problem "How many possible outcomes are there if a coin is flipped twice, a six-sided die is rolled three times, and a tetrahedral (four-sided) die is rolled once?" :question/worked-solution "For the coin, there are $2$ outcomes: \"Heads\" and \"Tails\".\n\nFor the six-sided die, there is a total of $6$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\nFor the tetrahedral die, there is a total of $4$ outcomes: $1,2,3,4.$\n\nUsing the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total Possible Outcomes} & =\\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the coin is flipped the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the first time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the second time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the six-sided die is rolled the third time} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of outcomes when the tetrahedral die is rolled the one time} \\\\  & =2\\times 2\\times 6\\times 6\\times 6\\times 4 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\times {6}^{3}\\times 4 \\\\  & =4\\times 216\\times 4 \\\\  & =3\\,456.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $3\\,456$ possible outcomes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6699397d-0014-5749-b439-4a8486990986" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93c2045e-9290-57b2-86ba-0eb7df87627c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270537} {:answer/id #uuid "255ea578-d3d3-5a18-922a-0da0b8ad13f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270538} {:answer/id #uuid "896180fc-999a-5702-b8e9-efd62ba9227f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270539} {:answer/id #uuid "372aea3b-eaf1-5930-8038-1e6d9ed73323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270540} {:answer/id #uuid "a892f6ff-86b2-5b0b-a4f4-7b6092b08a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "255ea578-d3d3-5a18-922a-0da0b8ad13f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270538} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4ea975-9d9e-5269-a910-9269cecdd802" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274383}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92ff5704-f3f3-59c9-aa47-1a38090b6514" :question/math-academy-id "q-123598" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${210}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd8f5a2-af38-5f9b-b4ef-00527ed0b4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270547} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f9f8bc-16e8-5d9b-b5a9-45ee4193d56a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270548} {:answer/id #uuid "be3c5c76-eeed-5b5e-a403-6c7dcc44904b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270549} {:answer/id #uuid "f82df0d7-9f91-5387-ade2-97e41a7f2763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270550} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8c3622-4945-5a09-af9b-c6943fccca14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec8c3622-4945-5a09-af9b-c6943fccca14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270551} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c1bf3d1-b522-5191-b582-5664e71efcf9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274385}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b889e158-30a8-50db-9eb5-59129862f7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-123602" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0052499-42ab-5818-a844-20eb099c3f7b" 